

By default, Transformers.js uses [hosted pretrained models](https://huggingface.co/models?library=transformers.js) and [precompiled WASM binaries](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@huggingface/transformers@4.0.1/dist/), which should work out-of-the-box. You can customize this as follows:

### Settings

```javascript
import { env } from '@huggingface/transformers';

// Specify a custom location for models (defaults to '/models/').
env.localModelPath = '/path/to/models/';

// Disable the loading of remote models from the Hugging Face Hub:
env.allowRemoteModels = false;

// Set location of .wasm files. Defaults to use a CDN.
env.backends.onnx.wasm.wasmPaths = '/path/to/files/';
```

For a full list of available settings, check out the [API Reference](./api/env).

### Convert your models to ONNX

We recommend using [Optimum](https://github.com/huggingface/optimum-onnx) to convert your PyTorch models to ONNX in a single command. For the full list of supported architectures, check out the [Optimum documentation](https://huggingface.co/docs/optimum-onnx/onnx/overview).

### Benchmark cache implementations

If you want to benchmark generation caches, prefer the lower-level model API over `pipeline()`. This gives you direct access to `generate()` options like `cache_implementation`, `past_key_values`, and `cache_stats`.

```javascript
import { AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer } from '@huggingface/transformers';

const modelId = 'onnx-community/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct';
const tokenizer = await AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(modelId);
const model = await AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(modelId, {
  dtype: 'q4',
  device: 'webgpu',
});

const messages = [
  { role: 'system', content: 'You are a helpful assistant.' },
  { role: 'user', content: 'Tell me a funny joke.' },
];

const prompt = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(messages, {
  tokenize: true,
  add_generation_prompt: true,
  return_dict: true,
});

const baseline = await model.generate({
  ...prompt,
  max_new_tokens: 128,
  do_sample: false,
  return_dict_in_generate: true,
  cache_implementation: 'dynamic',
});

const turbo = await model.generate({
  ...prompt,
  max_new_tokens: 128,
  do_sample: false,
  return_dict_in_generate: true,
  cache_implementation: 'turboquant',
});

console.log('baseline cache', baseline.cache_stats);
console.log('turbo cache', turbo.cache_stats);
```

For repeated comparisons from the repo itself, use:

```bash
pnpm --dir packages/transformers benchmark:turboquant
```

You can override the defaults with environment variables such as `MODEL_ID`, `DEVICE`, `DTYPE`, `MAX_NEW_TOKENS`, `RUNS`, and `PROMPT`.

If you benchmark with `DEVICE=webgpu` from Node.js, note that ONNX Runtime WebGPU can still fail in native code before JavaScript sees an exception. If that happens, rerun with `DEVICE=cpu` to validate the harness itself, then measure WebGPU in a browser environment.
